{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/sparse-marker-frame-and-grid-validation.md", "content": "# Sparse marker frames and exact grid validation

In one sparse-marker task, demonstrations showed output color 2 added along an axis-aligned rectangular perimeter determined by the extrema of marker positions, with original markers preserved and the rectangle's interior left unchanged. The initial hypotheses—vertical gap filling and filling every row/column span—were wrong. Treat frame construction as a candidate, not a default rule: confirm each edge and non-edge against all demonstrations, especially where marker positions extend beyond one another. For a candidate frame, compute min/max row and column over the relevant markers, color only the perimeter cells, and preserve existing markers.

For large outputs, generate the grid mechanically and validate dimensions and palette before submission. Validate the final action payload after composing it: every row must have exactly the input's expected width, the row count must match, and values must be legal integers. A transcription/serialization width error makes an otherwise plausible grid invalid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/sparse-marker-frame-and-grid-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/repeated-motif-extension.md", "content": "# Repeated motif, continuation, and extension: review notes

A single demonstration showed a structured foreground motif repeated in a second location; only a region at the later instance's edge changed to color 1. Other demonstrations showed the same broad palette but dissimilar geometry and localized changed regions. This does not establish a reliable shared operation by itself: the hypothesis that color 1 simply fills a horizontal run from the rightmost marks failed on the test, as did a second attempt that filled apparent gaps between sparse cells.

For future analogous tasks, compare exact repeated foreground masks before interpreting each changed run independently. Test whether copies differ by a precise template completion, periodic continuation, or directional extension, and verify the candidates against all demos and negative evidence. Do not use these session examples as a solved rule. After an incorrect grid, discard the complete inferred transformation and reconstruct deltas anew. Spend demos on an explicit discriminator and retain the exact one-action JSON contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/repeated-motif-extension.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassess after an incorrect ARC submission

A wrong submission is evidence against the transformation hypothesis as a whole. Do not respond by changing a few guessed cells while preserving an unverified premise. Re-read the full demonstrations and list plausible rule families, then compare complete-grid predictions coordinate-by-coordinate, including untouched background, marker retention, orientation, and extent. If the remaining hypotheses predict different outputs on a new example, spend a demo request; otherwise submit only the best-supported complete prediction.

For sparse puzzles combining a motif and separate marker cells, explicitly test whether the motif is geometrically transformed around/at markers (reflection, rotation, projection) in addition to translation or replication. Validate the candidate transformation against all demo coordinates before extrapolating.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/global-propagation-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessing apparent propagation in noisy grids

A distinctive colored cluster embedded in a dense random-looking binary field can make a fill/propagation hypothesis feel plausible, but visual proximity is not enough. Before applying a flood fill, replay it against every demo and compare the full predicted set with all recolored and untouched cells. Test 4- and 8-connectivity separately, and check whether candidate traversable cells are actually limited to one background value. If either model predicts extensive recoloring that the demonstration does not show, reject the flood-fill premise; do not submit a broad fill merely because the test has marker cells.

For costly demo requests, make the uncertainty actionable: state the remaining candidate rule families and identify an observable difference a new pair can resolve. If the evidence remains underdetermined, obtain another example rather than making successive speculative submissions. After any incorrect answer, reconsider the whole transformation, not just individual coordinates.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/global-propagation-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessment after an incorrect stream submission

An incorrect answer falsifies the complete inferred transformation, not merely a few predicted cells. Before the next action, replay each demonstration from its original input and output, recording all changed cells and meaningful unchanged candidates. Identify what the failed prediction assumed and which demonstration evidence rules that assumption out. Do not reuse the prior output with a few cell edits unless the demos independently establish that local correction.

For the next costly demo, name concrete competing hypotheses and the input feature whose possible forms would cause different outputs. If the hypotheses do not produce different test predictions, a demo has no decision value; if they do, request only when a new pair could distinguish them. Keep the one-action-per-turn contract, and only submit after the reconstructed rule predicts changes and non-changes throughout all available pairs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/enlarged-grid-copy-validation.md", "content": "# Validate enlarged-grid continuation hypotheses

When an output is larger than its input and preserves the input in one subregion, do not infer that the remaining area is tiled or mirrored by default. Extract corresponding blocks from every demo and compare exact cells against direct copies, flips, rotations, and any stated periodic rule. Keep the preserved region and generated region conceptually separate. A visually unrelated added region is evidence against naive repetition, not a license to invent arbitrary noise; seek a deterministic relation supported by all examples before submitting. Use demos only when they can distinguish named candidates, and do not extrapolate from output dimensions alone.
